# Felkyo Creatures — Git attributes.
#
# WHAT THIS IS FOR: two jobs. Keeping line endings consistent between Windows and
# the Linux production host, and deciding which files (if any) are stored via
# Git LFS rather than directly in Git.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THE SITE'S ART IS *NOT* IN LFS — read this before adding it back
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The creature sprites, the logo and the 404 artwork used to be stored in Git LFS,
# on the reasoning that binary art would bloat the repository. Two things turned
# out to be true, and together they reversed that decision:
#
# 1. THE ART IS TINY. All of it together is about 1 MB, and the largest single
#    file is under 700 KB. That is nothing to Git. LFS solves a problem this
#    project does not have, at the cost of a tool everyone has to install and
#    understand before they can even see the pictures.
#
# 2. LFS BREAKS THE DEPLOYMENT. Railway does not fetch LFS files when it builds.
#    It would receive the little text pointers instead of the images, and every
#    creature and the logo would be broken on the live site — with a confusing
#    cause, because the files look perfectly fine on your own machine.
#
# So all art the *website* serves is stored as ordinary files in Git. If somebody
# later adds art back into LFS, the live site's images will silently break.
#
# LFS is still configured below for genuinely large SOURCE files (.psd), which are
# working files for the artist, are potentially huge, and are never served by the
# website — so nothing breaks if a build does not fetch them.

# Artist source files only. These can be very large and the site never serves them.
*.psd   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# Keep line endings sane across Windows and the Linux production host:
# text files are normalised to LF in the repository.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Image formats are binary: never convert their line endings, or they corrupt.
# (They are stored as ordinary Git files — see the note above.)
*.gif   binary
*.png   binary
*.apng  binary
*.webp  binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
